# SEO and Content QA

Apply when editing `scripts/seo/`, `scripts/content/`, `scripts/run-seo.ts`, `scripts/run-content.ts`, or the `seo` / `content` sections of `qa.config.json`.

## Technical SEO is not a ranking audit

`npm run test:seo` observes metadata and GET behavior: title, meta description, canonical, Open Graph, image alt, robots.txt, sitemap.xml, URL consistency, duplicate-URL indicators, 404, and redirects.

Do **not**:

- Invent a preferred trailing-slash, www, or scheme policy
- Judge a redirect destination correct unless `qa.config.json` (or the local fixture probe) names it
- Treat a passing run as “the site will rank”
- Replace discovery `analyzeSeo` — extend it; keep Layer 2.11 honest

## Content QA is not fact-checking

`npm run test:content` finds missing headings, missing/empty content, empty sections, obvious placeholders (`lorem ipsum`, `TODO`, `TBD`, `FIXME`, “your text here”), missing alt, and broken same-origin images/links.

Do **not** mark marketing copy, prices, or “best/leading” claims true or false unless `content.expectedValues` provides an authoritative expected string.

## Execution

```bash
npm run test:seo
npm run test:content
```

Reports: `reports/seo/`, `reports/content/`. Enterprise report sections 2.11 and 2.15.

Never hide failures. A fixture missing meta/canonical/OG or planted content issues is a real observation.
